<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ibn Tufayl</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1105–<a href="/years/1185">1185</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Abubacer</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ibn Tufayl was an Andalusian philosopher, physician and court official of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a>, born near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>. Serving as physician and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-yaqub-yusuf-i">Abu Yaqub Yusuf I</a>, he introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/averroes">Averroes</a> to the court and encouraged him to write his commentaries on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>. His philosophical novel Hayy ibn Yaqzan tells of a man who reaches knowledge of God through reason alone on a desert island.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ibn-tufayl.jpg" alt="Ibn Tufayl" width="320" height="343" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ibn_%E1%B9%ACufail,_Sayr_mulhimah_min_al-Sharq_wa-al-Gharb.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
